    "problem_statement": "File overwrite behavior is inverted\n\nDescription\n\nWhen writing to a file using the FileWriter class, the overwrite parameter behavior is inverted. Setting overwrite=True results in appending to the file, while setting overwrite=False results in overwriting the file.\n\nFor example, given the following code:\n\n```python\nimport pysnooper\n\n# First call with overwrite=True should create a new file\npysnooper.snoop(output='my_log.txt', overwrite=True)(lambda: print('first'))()\n\n# Second call with overwrite=False should append to the file\npysnooper.snoop(output='my_log.txt', overwrite=False)(lambda: print('second'))()\n```\n\nThe expected behavior is that the first call creates a new file and the second call appends to it. However, the actual behavior is the opposite - the first call appends (creating the file if it doesn't exist) and the second call overwrites the file, losing the first output.\n\nThis affects all file output operations where the overwrite parameter is used."
